On Tue, 25 Nov 2008 21:06:31 -0500, "EMK"
<noneofyourbusiness@columbus.rr.com> scribed into the ether:

>1.  Are there "safe" methods of hoarding stuff?  Are certain locations (e.g. 
>rooms only accessible by opening doors) more secure than others?  Are there 
>particularly "unsafe" paces to put things (e.g. areas crawling with 
>humanoids rather than less intelligent monsters)?  Other than being looted 
>by NPCs, is my pile of goodies in any danger of disappearing or degrading 
>over time?

Except for corpses, items only  change when an exterior force acts on them,
be it you, your pet, or a monster.

There are several ways to keep your stuff safe, it depends on just how much
effort you want to go through to do it.

The absolute safest way is to dig a pit, drop all your stuff inside, and
drop a boulder on top of it to seal it back up....although I'm not sure
what would happen if a monster zapped a wand of digging down on that
square. That would be the only risk. No creature has any way to get to
items stored like that by itself.

Generally, inside of a box/chest, and sitting on top of a burned (not
etched) Elbereth will keep your items safe from damn near everything.
Monsters don't ever loot chests, and most monsters will avoid any square
with Elbereth and an item on top of it. For the safest route, make the
container an ice box, since this is the only contain that cubes cannot
digest.

>2.  I've read that it's a good idea to put potions and fragile objects in 
>containers.  So far I've only come across chests and boxes (the former don't 
>let me travel diagonally around boulders when I carry them); are there good 
>uses for these bigger containers? (e.g. #1)

There are 3 kinds of bags that might appear in the game. They are a lot
more portable than boxes and chests. Boxes and chests are convenient for
stashes, but much too heavy for carrying around on an extended basis.

>3.  Is there any way to check on the status of my pet?  I'd like to see how 
>my little buddy is progressing.

Wand of probing, stethoscope, a probe effect from Magicbane (which involves
hitting your pet...not a good route).

>4.  Is there any way to get a sense of how much damage I am dealing or how 
>injured my foe is?  I think I remember that healers can do that, but what 
>about other classes?

Probing and stethoscope can tell you how many HP you've inflicted on a
target, as well as how many it has left. The only other cue is if a monster
starts running, then it's probably low on HP.



